kantJitArgT -- SEQ 666, with kantJitT: the C++ walk's S1b action (newLocalArgT's nl3an) and a refusal inside a body.
NL AL-3a n: nl3an(2) through the door reads 0 1 2 with no refusal, as its plain run does. The door call is not a call
of the action, so nothing binds nl3an's argument: the driver binds it from kant (nl3an.argument := kjTwo), and that
works only after one plain call has minted the argument slot (NL PRIME) -- runAction mints and flags it, and kant has
no spelling for the flag. Unprimed, lv3a copies a holder and the row reads 2 with one refusal (measured).
NL KR: kr1 run plainly and kr2 (the same body) through the door. The holder arithmetic refuses once in each, the
statement after it does not run in either, and the driver goes on.
